<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="rhody2">

  <xacro:arg name="namespace" default="rhody" />
  <xacro:property name="namespace" value="$(arg namespace)" scope="global" />
  <xacro:property name="deg" value="${pi / 180.0}" />

  <!-- fov:=true adds visual-only sensor field-of-view volumes (and the planned
       Water Linked 3D sonar frame) for figures and coverage inspection. Off by
       default so the runtime link/TF set is unchanged for SLAM, nvblox and the
       EKF. See sensor_fov.xacro. -->
  <xacro:arg name="fov" default="false" />

  <!-- base_link follows REP-103: x forward, y port, z up. -->

  <!-- ==================================================================
       base_link is the USBL (Subsonus transducer).

       Two sets of numbers feed this file.

       1. Tape measurements on the vehicle, 2026-10-12 (+/-1-2 cm; several
          were taken twice). These place the SPRINT-Nav, the Nucleus and the
          iLBL directly in the USBL frame:
            SPRINT-Nav -> USBL     17 cm forward (*), 44 cm up, same y
            SPRINT-Nav -> Nucleus  31 cm forward, 1 cm down, same y
            USBL -> iLBL           5 cm aft, 28 cm port, z not measured
          (*) The field note reads "35x-18x", taken here as 35 - 18 = 17 cm.
              The bottom-plate photo puts the USBL 16-24 cm forward of the
              SPRINT-Nav, so it is plausible. *** CONFIRM ***

       2. The Shapr3D model, measured relative to the Nucleus DVL in its
          ORIGINAL design position (the round cutout aft of where the head
          now sits), because base_link used to be there (as on Rhody 1).
          The Gemini and Voyis still come from this chain, shifted into the
          USBL frame by usbl_to_cad_dvl_*: the CAD's USBL -> DVL offset,
          dX 3.0246 in forward, dZ 12.1969 in below. That is NOT where the
          Nucleus is now. The head moved on 2026-10-12 and the cameras did
          not, so the two must stay decoupled.

       The two sets disagree on where that original Nucleus position is:
       CAD (7.7, 0, -31) cm vs tape (-1, +6, -41.5) cm from the USBL. The z
       gap is about the Subsonus's own height, so the CAD point is probably
       the USBL base and the tape the transducer; the x/y gap is not
       explained. Until USBL -> Voyis (or Gemini) is tape-measured directly,
       the camera and sonar extrinsics are only good to ~10 cm against the
       DVLs and USBL.
       TODO: tape USBL -> Voyis left camera (x, y, z) and re-anchor the CAD
       chain on it.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="usbl_to_cad_dvl_x" value="0.0768"  scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="usbl_to_cad_dvl_z" value="-0.3098" scope="global"/>

  <!-- Tape, 2026-10-12: USBL -> SPRINT-Nav. The Nucleus and the iLBL below
       are measured from these, so a corrected reading changes one place. -->
  <xacro:property name="sprintnav_x" value="-0.17" scope="global"/>  <!-- 35 - 18 cm; CONFIRM -->
  <xacro:property name="sprintnav_y" value="0.0"   scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="sprintnav_z" value="-0.44" scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="nucleus_fwd_of_sprintnav" value="0.31"/>
  <xacro:property name="nucleus_below_sprintnav"  value="0.01"/>

  <!-- Base Link -->
  <link name="base_link">
    <!-- rhody2.dae is converted from rhody2.glb (Blender export) with assimp;
         Humble's RViz segfaults loading .glb directly, so keep the Collada. -->
    <visual>
      <!-- The export is Y-up (Blender glTF conversion baked into the root
           node); -90 deg about X brings it back to REP-103 z-up. -->
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-pi/2} 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://rhody/meshes/rhody2.dae"/>
      </geometry>
    </visual>
  </link>

  <!-- USBL (Subsonus): base_link origin is here, so translation is zero. Kept
       as its own link so sensor data names the device rather than base_link.

       ORIENTATION: this frame IS the device's output axes, per REP-145 - the
       driver publishes body vectors exactly as reported. Advanced Navigation's
       body frame is forward +X, right +Y, up -Z (Subsonus manual 9.1.3.3), i.e.
       FRD, a 180 deg roll from base_link. The subsea unit mounts on top of the
       vehicle pointing at the surface (7.3.2); with the web UI's Alignment
       Configuration (9.1.3.5) set to Orientation=Subsea and the alignment
       offsets correct, the device re-expresses its output in the VEHICLE's
       FRD axes regardless of how the housing sits, which is what this assumes.
       *** PROVISIONAL - confirm on the bench: *** with the vehicle level and
       still, imu_raw.linear_acceleration.z reads about -9.8 if this frame is
       right (z down, so gravity's reaction is -z) and about +9.8 if the
       device is actually outputting z-up, in which case this roll is wrong.
       Also confirm the web UI Orientation is "Subsea", not "Surface". -->
  <link name="usbl_link"/>
  <joint name="base_to_usbl" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="usbl_link"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${pi} 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Inverted LBL (iLBL) transducer. An independent sensor, not part of the
       Subsonus: it ranges to seabed beacons and reports a world-referenced
       position, published as NavSatFix on /rhody/nav/sensors/ilbl/fix with
       frame_id ilbl_link (config/ilbl.yaml). This link is where that fix is
       referenced on the vehicle, i.e. the antenna offset a navsat_transform
       would apply. No body vectors are published, so no device-axes rotation
       is declared; identity to base_link.

       Tape, 2026-10-12: 5 cm aft and 28 cm to port of the USBL (seen head-on
       from the bow the iLBL is aft and to the viewer's right, which is port).
       It mounts on the black plate beside the USBL on the top deck.
       *** PROVISIONAL z: *** not measured, assumed level with the USBL. -->
  <link name="ilbl_link"/>
  <joint name="base_to_ilbl" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="ilbl_link"/>
    <origin xyz="-0.05 0.28 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Nortek Nucleus 1000 DVL. Named to match what nucleus_driver_ros2
       actually publishes (frame_id: nucleus_dvl) and the sensor_fov.yaml
       section and meshes/fov/nucleus_dvl_*.stl, rather than the old generic
       dvl_link - there are two DVLs on the vehicle, so "the DVL" is ambiguous.

       Origin: tape, 2026-10-12, from the SPRINT-Nav: 31 cm forward, 1 cm
       lower (the Nucleus sits proud of the bottom plate, the SPRINT-Nav is
       recessed), same y. The head was moved that day from the CAD position
       (the round cutout aft of it): 15.5 cm forward, 6 cm to starboard and
       4 cm lower. usbl_to_cad_dvl_* still names the old position, on purpose.

       ORIENTATION: this frame IS the instrument's own axes, per REP-145 - the
       driver publishes every topic exactly as the device reports it, and this
       declaration is what makes that correct on the vehicle. Nortek's
       instrument frame when mounted downward-facing (N3015-033 figure 4) is
       x-forward, y-starboard, z-down, which is a 180 deg roll from base_link
       (x-forward, y-port, z-up). That assumes the head is fitted with its
       X-axis pointing forward, which Nortek recommends but does not require
       (section 4.3). Mounting yaw confirmed from the 2026-10-12 bottom-plate
       photo: the engraved X arrow points away from the connector, and the
       connector points aft at the SPRINT-Nav (figure 4: connector exits
       along -X), so X is forward; the Y arrow points to starboard when
       viewed from below, as FRD requires. -->
  <link name="nucleus_dvl"/>
  <joint name="base_to_nucleus" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="nucleus_dvl"/>
    <origin xyz="${sprintnav_x + nucleus_fwd_of_sprintnav} ${sprintnav_y} ${sprintnav_z - nucleus_below_sprintnav}"
            rpy="${pi} 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Sonardyne SPRINT-Nav Mini INS/DVL, the second DVL aboard for the
       comparison testing. Bottom-mounted, recessed in the plate, aft of the
       Nucleus and on the same lateral line as it and the USBL.

       Origin: tape, 2026-10-12 (sprintnav_* above): 17 cm aft of and 44 cm
       below the USBL, same y. The 17 is "35 - 18" in the field notes and
       needs confirming; the z and y are direct readings.

       The lever arm to the Nucleus is now 31 cm, not the 20 cm that was
       guessed before: under rotation the two heads legitimately differ by
       omega x r, ~5.4 cm/s at 0.31 m and 10 deg/s, the same order as DVL
       accuracy. Any DVL-vs-DVL comparison has to remove that first.

       Note: HNAV depth is reported at the vehicle CRP configured on the
       device web UI; HNAV altitude is at the DVL head (not attitude
       compensated).

       ORIENTATION: this frame IS the sensor's own axes, per REP-145 - the
       driver publishes body vectors exactly as HNAV reports them, which is
       x-forward, y-starboard, z-down (FRD), a 180 deg roll from base_link.
       Assumes the unit is fitted DVL-face down with its forward axis along
       the vehicle's, like the Nucleus. *** PROVISIONAL - confirm the
       mounting yaw on the vehicle *** (the face carries no visible forward
       mark; with the vehicle still, velocity.z and the HNAV status telegram
       should agree on sign with the Nucleus's, and a forward nudge must give
       +x). -->
  <link name="sprintnav_link"/>
  <joint name="base_to_sprintnav" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="sprintnav_link"/>
    <origin xyz="${sprintnav_x} ${sprintnav_y} ${sprintnav_z}" rpy="${pi} 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- FLS (Gemini): measured in Shapr3D from sonar to the DVL's CAD position
       (see usbl_to_cad_dvl_* at the top; not the Nucleus's current spot):
       dX 14.9247 in, dY 6.0827 in, dZ 8.7819 in (0.3791, 0.1545, 0.2231 m).
       Forward of the DVL, port of centerline, above the DVL.
       5 deg down pitch.
       Named to match the driver's frame_id (gemini_fls) and the
       sensor_fov.yaml section, rather than the old fls_link. -->
  <link name="gemini_fls"/>
  <joint name="base_to_gemini" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="gemini_fls"/>
    <origin xyz="${0.3791 + usbl_to_cad_dvl_x} 0.1545 ${0.2231 + usbl_to_cad_dvl_z}"
            rpy="0 ${5.0 * deg} 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Camera optical frame: z forward, x right, y down (REP-103 / ISAAC ROS) -->
  <xacro:macro name="optical_frame" params="name parent">
    <link name="${name}"/>
    <joint name="${parent}_to_${name}" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${parent}"/>
      <child link="${name}"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-pi/2} 0 ${-pi/2}"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- Voyis Stereo: measured in Shapr3D from left camera to the DVL's CAD
       position (shifted into the USBL frame by usbl_to_cad_dvl_*, like the
       Gemini; see the note at the top on how well that chain is anchored):
       dX 12.0228 in, dZ 3.5748 in (0.3054, 0.0908 m). The stereo pair is
       centered on the vehicle centerline, so each camera sits at
       +/- baseline/2 in y. voyis_baseline must match the factory stereo
       calibration (SDK ExtrinsicCalibrationData_t::stereo_baseline; dump it
       with SaveCalibrationDataApp).
       Value below is the factory baseline for head 180114031, read from the
       survey export's Voyis/Calibration_180114031/AgisoftSlaveOffsets.json
       (x = 0.16969684810099406) and corroborated by colmap_rig.json. -->
  <xacro:property name="voyis_baseline" value="0.16969684810099406"/>
  <xacro:property name="voyis_x" value="0.3054"/>
  <xacro:property name="voyis_z" value="0.0908"/>

  <!-- Voyis Stereo: Left Entrance Pupil (port side) -->
  <link name="voyis_left_link"/>
  <joint name="base_to_voyis_left" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="voyis_left_link"/>
    <origin xyz="${voyis_x + usbl_to_cad_dvl_x} ${voyis_baseline/2} ${voyis_z + usbl_to_cad_dvl_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <xacro:optical_frame name="voyis_left_optical" parent="voyis_left_link"/>

  <!-- Voyis Stereo: Right Entrance Pupil (starboard side) -->
  <link name="voyis_right_link"/>
  <joint name="base_to_voyis_right" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="voyis_right_link"/>
    <origin xyz="${voyis_x + usbl_to_cad_dvl_x} ${-voyis_baseline/2} ${voyis_z + usbl_to_cad_dvl_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <xacro:optical_frame name="voyis_right_optical" parent="voyis_right_link"/>

  <!-- Voyis IMU: inside the camera housing. Placed at the left camera link
       as an identity placeholder — TODO: replace with the factory extrinsics
       (SDK ImuCalibration_t::body_T_imu, dump with SaveCalibrationDataApp),
       including the IMU axis orientation. -->
  <link name="voyis_imu"/>
  <joint name="voyis_left_to_imu" type="fixed">
    <parent link="voyis_left_link"/>
    <child link="voyis_imu"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Sensor coverage volumes. Included last so it can attach to every sensor
       link declared above. -->
  <xacro:if value="$(arg fov)">
    <!-- Relative, not $(find rhody): lets the figure renderer expand this file
         straight from the source tree without sourcing the workspace. -->
    <xacro:include filename="sensor_fov.xacro"/>
  </xacro:if>

</robot>
